WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.450
Critics love it, audiences love it — heck,
everybody loves it.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.139
But there are still a few John Wick moments
that have fans baying for blood.

00:00:08.139 --> 00:00:10.950
Warning: spoilers ahead for John Wick: Chapter
4.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:16.289
John Wick: Chapter 4 ups the stakes early
on by killing off a fan-favorite recurring

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:20.936
character: Charon, the cool, collected concierge
of the New York Continental.

00:00:20.936 --> 00:00:23.509
"How good to see you so soon, Mr. Wick."

00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:27.880
What makes this moment even more shocking
is the fact that Charon is coldly executed

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.470
in retaliation for a problem that is entirely
outside of his control.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.520
He essentially dies to make sure we understand
our new villain, the Marquis Vincent de Gramont,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.230
is a really bad dude.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.440
After years of faithful service in the name
of the High Table, Charon goes out as collateral

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.750
damage — and it's all because Winston doesn't
hit John Wick with a fatal shot at the end

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:47.604
of the last movie.

00:00:47.604 --> 00:00:49.154
It's harsh, to say the least.

00:00:49.154 --> 00:00:53.460
This death hits especially hard, as John Wick:
Chapter 4 landed in theaters right on the

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.770
heels of Lance Reddick's sudden death at the
age of 60.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.350
This adds an unfortunate "too soon" cloud
over the story beat.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.540
As with many of Reddick's best roles, the
actor brings a calm and clear-eyed gravitas

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:09.119
to Charon that cements the Continental as
an authoritative edifice of power and meaning.

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:13.590
He also plays fantastically off the expressive,
wry tones of Ian McShane's performance.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:18.900
It's a tremendous shame that John Wick: Chapter
4 will be his last appearance as the character:

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.759
Reddick had been set to reprise the role in
the franchise's Ballerina spin-off movie.

00:01:23.027 --> 00:01:26.690
The first two sequels to John Wick basically
begin in media res.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.830
If you're not familiar, this is story-speak
for "mid fight scene"; these action-packed

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.500
features pick up the moment the prior movies
leave off.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.640
John Wick: Chapter 4, by contrast, starts
remarkably slow, and it takes the movie a

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.610
ponderous half-hour to set the table for the
first big fight scene at the Osaka Continental.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.740
Relative to the quick pace that's made the
franchise successful so far, this is a bold

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.750
gamble for the fourth movie to take.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.000
"Just have fun out there."

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.909
It's bolder still because it requires the
audience to have a better-than-cursory recall

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:55.990
of the John Wick timeline thus far.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.649
As some fans have pointed out, it's actually
kind of hard to recall the details of the

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:03.619
Wick-verse's archaic plot mechanics, even
for devoted fans.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:07.789
The first three movies primarily focus on
a multi-sensory barrage of fight scenes that

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:12.510
just don't leave much RAM in your mind to
grasp all the markers, tattoos, clans, and

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.750
odd bits of Latin.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.850
And, despite how long it takes to get going,
John Wick: Chapter 4 doesn't work too hard

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.519
to explain what's going on.

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:22.890
Some viewers might have to Google who the
dude in the desert John kills in the beginning

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.861
even is to understand what's at stake.

00:02:25.861 --> 00:02:29.000
The villains of the John Wick franchise take
many shapes and forms.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.600
Chapter 4 takes the path untrodden with the
Marquis, its main antagonist, who is mostly

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.040
frightening because of what he represents.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.530
Just like John Wick himself, the High Table
— of which the Marquis is a senior member

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.710
— is more of an idea that can't be killed
than an actual group of mortal people.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.790
The Marquis cements this by acting as the
sadistic personification of ancient forces

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.080
like greed and power.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.610
John Wick: Chapter 4 finds a particularly
brutal way to let us know the Marquis is more

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:59.140
twisted than usual: The vicious villain subjects
Mr. Nobody to a horrific test of will.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.019
Stabbing the bounty hunter through the hand,
the Marquis essentially demands that he pulls

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:06.530
his hand out through the knife's blade instead
of pulling the blade upwards.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.910
Mr. Nobody, apparently willing to do anything
for a shot at the John Wick contract, complies

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.550
in a squeamish bit of realistic effects work.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:17.360
Though it's a moment that fits perfectly with
the over-the-top John Wick style, it's more

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.680
visceral and intense than the franchise's
normal fists-and-guns melees — prompting

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.587
at least a few groans of disgust in theaters.

00:03:24.792 --> 00:03:28.920
In general, the John Wick movies rely more
on the poetry of their action sequences than

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.849
the dramatic tension of the narrative.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:34.610
This approach shows the most strain in John
Wick: Chapter 4, which slows down between

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:37.500
action set pieces to give us time to think.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.629
What this means, of course, is that audience
members are a lot more likely to think, "Wait,

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:43.070
what's actually going on here?"

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.730
For example, Caine and Mr. Nobody follow John
Wick around the entire movie.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.430
They're sort of trying to kill him, but they
always manage to let him go.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.610
At no point is this more confusing than when
they interrupt his face-off with Killa Harkan,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.130
a ruthless crime lord who killed his former
mentor.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:01.673
"You want to kill him, I want to kill him…

00:04:01.673 --> 00:04:04.170
What about you, Mr. Wick?"

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:09.090
It's unclear why either mercenary is waiting
so long to kill John Wick, or why John himself

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.629
is acting so coy about attacking Killa.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:15.120
In truth, the whole scene really just seems
like a thin excuse for him to end up with

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.570
the famous "dead man's hand" of aces and eights
during a poker game.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:23.169
It also contains one of the most confusing
Caine-related moments: Despite being blind,

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:25.350
he can tell exactly what his hand is.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.889
Star Wars fans might at least enjoy this unintentional
callback to Rogue One, in which actor Donnie

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:34.370
Yen also plays a blind character who can still
sort of see — but everyone else will be

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.370
more than a little lost.

00:04:35.912 --> 00:04:39.919
Perhaps in tacit acknowledgement that the
audience might be getting somewhat desensitized

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:44.620
to human-on-human violence after four movies,
John Wick: Chapter 4 throws in a very different

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.670
sort of heart-skipping moment, late in its
runtime.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:52.210
This scene goes back to the first movie's
potent well by placing a dog in jeopardy — although

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:55.539
this time, it's a thankfully brief moment
of canine peril.

00:04:55.539 --> 00:04:59.710
Mr. Nobody's faithful companion has more than
won us over by the time he gets rudely tossed

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.490
into a windshield by the Marquis' top henchman,
Chidi.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.690
Despite the fact that he all but shatters
the glass and bounces into the street, the

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.700
dog gets right back up and barks to let us
know he's okay.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.740
It's a sudden shock delivered during an otherwise
surreally choreographed scene set beside Paris'

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.080
Arc de Triomphe, and it hits hard.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.630
Some fans, perhaps still scarred from the
first movie, have even taken to platforms

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.360
like Reddit to make sure the dog lives before
they actually see the movie.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.856
Money makes the world go 'round, and that's
especially true in the John Wick universe.

00:05:28.856 --> 00:05:29.900
How do we know this?

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:34.400
By the fact that, as the bounty on John Wick
grows, hundreds upon hundreds of people decide

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.280
that the price is suddenly right to take him
on themselves, even though the many previous

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.610
attempts on the life of Baba Yaga have failed.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.720
Yet, there are still exceptions to this rule.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.800
For most of John Wick: Chapter 4, it seems
like Mr. Nobody is the rare character to realize this.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.800
He seems content to simply track John from
place to place and try to bilk the Marquis

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:53.040
for a finder's fee.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.180
But, late in the game, we learn that even
Mr. Nobody has a price: $40 million dollars;

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:00.780
after this, he goes all-out in an effort to
bag John.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.240
This happens after he gets the drop on the
unkillable protagonist multiple times — sadly,

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.050
Mr. Nobody loses a fair amount of intrigue
when we realize that he's just like people

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.970
who only play the lottery when it gets over
$1 billion.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:16.430
When he changes his mind again after John
saves his dog's life, it endears him to us,

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.233
but it doesn't make his character any more
coherent.

00:06:19.233 --> 00:06:22.937
"You ready, John?"

00:06:25.335 --> 00:06:26.325
"Yeah."

00:06:26.325 --> 00:06:31.370
Even before the release of John Wick: Chapter
4, fans pointed out that its 169 minute runtime

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:32.990
is seriously lengthy.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.070
This is a serious departure for the franchise:
The first three movies in the series endeared

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:40.750
themselves to fans, in part, by being sprints
instead of marathons.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:44.560
Even the biggest John Wick fan might feel
like the fourth installment needs to be shorter.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.720
It doesn't help that this feels like part
of a pernicious trend in modern cinema.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.849
All kinds of recent movies seem to be pushing
three hours these days, and the John Wick

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:56.139
formula doesn't lend itself too well to a
bloated runtime.

00:06:56.139 --> 00:06:59.940
The movie's length really puts a strain on
the scenes between action sequences, which

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.810
mostly consist of characters explaining made-up
High Table rules that are kinda hard to follow.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.690
Besides, who wants to miss a killer action
scene because their bladder just can't take it?

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.352
Give us an intermission next time!

00:07:10.766 --> 00:07:13.709
Yes, you'd better believe it: John Wick dies
at the end.

00:07:13.709 --> 00:07:17.640
The same John Wick we've watched kill thousands
of people in a few days over the course of

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.759
the first three movies.

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:24.398
The same John Wick who easily survived getting
hit by four cars and falling down 500 concrete steps.

00:07:24.398 --> 00:07:28.648
The unconquerable, merciless Baba Yaga, whose
name is in the title of every movie.

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:32.740
Our protagonist's death in what might be the
final chapter of the main franchise has fans

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.530
shocked and divided.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:39.030
Some feel like it undermines the whole story,
while others are hoping it's a total fake-out.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:42.900
After so much violence and death, it really
does feel like John Wick's defining character

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:46.169
trait is his ability to survive in spite of
impossible odds.

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:49.670
In a way, he exists more as a force of nature
than a man.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:53.539
It's mildly satisfying that his actions help
Caine become free of the High Table and allow

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:57.970
Winston to get the Continental restored — but
for John, the outcome is no different than

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.539
if he'd died at any of the hundreds of earlier
points in the saga when someone has tried

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:02.539
to kill him.

00:08:02.539 --> 00:08:06.470
With no one left to avenge, there's a strange
and potent kind of beauty in John Wick surviving

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:07.530
for his own sake.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.379
His apparent death, in contrast, seems to
support the Marquis' more nihilistic view.

00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:16.020
He sees John as essentially defined by death,
and according to this movie, that may be true

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.400
— and it could be the final word on the
matter.

00:08:18.868 --> 00:08:23.129
John Wick: Chapter 4 attempts to come full
circle as John sits dying on the steps of

00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:24.106
the Sacré-Cœur.

00:08:24.106 --> 00:08:28.590
His life flashes before his eyes, and we see
him with his wife as he lingers on their brief

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:29.699
and lovely marriage.

00:08:29.699 --> 00:08:34.209
But, as some fans have been quick to point
out, Daisy, the adorable beagle his wife left

00:08:34.209 --> 00:08:38.070
him after her death — the dog whose murder
draws him back into the assassin world in

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.570
the first place — is nowhere to be seen.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.840
"Yeah, I'm thinking I'm back!"

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.580
Now, we mean no offense to the memory of Helen
Wick.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.240
John loved her immensely.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.640
But Daisy deserves to be in the montage as
well.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:53.870
John cared so much for her that he kicked
off an entire cinematic saga in response to

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:55.060
her untimely demise.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:59.149
Seeing her fail to appear in the montage is
kind of like watching the Oscars snub people

00:08:59.149 --> 00:09:01.807
by leaving them out of the "in memoriam" segment.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:06.680
John Wick: Chapter 4 is the first film in
the John Wick franchise to feature an after-credits

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:11.480
scene, much to the consternation of many fans
who, not expecting one, left the theater early.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.880
It's disappointing, from a certain angle,
to see a movie franchise once confident enough

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:21.100
in its own appeal to eschew such ending teases
of future events suddenly bow to the trend.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:24.650
It's also kind of a big ask to make of an
audience that just sat through the longest

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:25.880
John Wick movie yet.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.950
Moreover, the fans who do make it all the
way might be disappointed, as the scene itself

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.420
is maddeningly brief.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:35.269
Akira, unsurprisingly, has tracked Caine down
to avenge her father's death.

00:09:35.269 --> 00:09:40.020
In a public square, she approaches a seemingly
oblivious Caine and whips out a knife — but

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.430
the screen cuts to black, leaving both of
their fates unknown.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.950
This is frustrating enough on its own, but
John Wick: Chapter 4 also disappoints grief-stricken

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:51.770
fans who might have hoped that a post-credits
scene would hint that John Wick isn't really

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:52.678
dead after all.

